 can only be the four combinational 32-bit `/` and `%` array dividers\n  in alu.sv (signed/unsigned quotient and remainder). They also explain the 12.3K LUT4\n  count. The three separate 64-bit multipliers (mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su) add area and\n  an extra operand-mux layer on top of that. Every other path in this 5-stage core\n  (forwarding mux -> adder -> redirect, BSRAM read -> load align) is an order of\n  magnitude shorter. CoreMark's timed region executes essentially no DIV/REM\n  (the compiler turns constant divisions into multiply-high at -O3, and the printf/\n  setup code that divides sits outside the start/stop MMIO markers), so an\n  iterative divider costs ~0 cycles of IPC while removing the dominant critical path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Take DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU out of the combinational ALU and execute them in a small\n  radix-2 sequential divider that stalls the pipeline in EX for ~34 cycles. Fmax\n  should then be set by the single 33x33 multiplier / forwarding / adder paths\n  (tens of ns instead of ~200 ns), i.e. a several-fold Fmax gain at essentially\n  unchanged cycles-per-iteration. Concretely:\n\n  1. New rtl/divider.sv (`divider`). Inputs: clock, reset, `start` (a DIV-class op is\n     in EX and EX/MEM is not dmem-stalled), `a`, `b` (post-forwarding rs1/rs2),\n     `is_signed`, `want_rem`. Outputs: `busy`, `done`, `result[31:0]`, plus\n     `a_q`/`b_q` (the raw latched operands, RVFI-only so synthesis prunes them from\n     the timed netlist). FSM: IDLE -> (cycle 0 latch raw a/b/op into registers; do NOT\n     negate here so the forwarding path is not lengthened) -> PREP (1 cycle: compute\n     |a|, |b| from the registered raw values, clear remainder, record result signs)\n     -> RUN (32 cycles of restoring shift/subtract: rem = {rem[30:0], q[31]};\n     if rem >= div then rem -= div, q = {q[30:0],1} else q = {q[30:0],0}; one\n     33-bit subtract + mux per cycle) -> FIX (1 cycle: conditional negate of quotient\n     or remainder, plus the b==0 and INT_MIN/-1 special cases: DIV by 0 -> all ones,\n     DIVU by 0 -> all ones, REM/REMU by 0 -> dividend, DIV INT_MIN/-1 -> INT_MIN,\n     REM INT_MIN/-1 -> 0; the plain restoring algorithm already yields q=all-ones and\n     r=a for b==0 on unsigned magnitudes, but the sign fix-up must be bypassed) ->\n     DONE (result valid, busy=0; return to IDLE when the instruction advances out of\n     EX, i.e. !stall_ex_mem). The latched operands make the result immune to the\n     forwarding selects changing while EX is parked (the producer retires while the\n     DIV waits, so fwd_sel falls to 0 and the stale ID/EX regfile value would be wrong).\n     Wrap the whole FSM body in `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` -> under ALTOPS\n     `busy = 0`, `done = 0` and the ALU's existing ALTOPS (a-b)^const stand-ins stay\n     in force, so riscv-formal sees exactly the same single-cycle behaviour as today.\n\n  2. rtl/alu.sv: in the non-ALTOPS branch remove the four `/` `%` expressions (DIV\n     ops fall through to `default: out = 0`; ex_stage substitutes the divider result).\n     Keep the ALTOPS branch bit-identical. Replace the three multipliers by ONE\n     signed 33x33 multiply: `a_ext = {a_signed & a[31], a}`, `b_ext = {b_signed & b[31], b}`\n     with a_signed = (op==MULH || op==MULHSU) and b_signed = (op==MULH); take\n     prod[31:0] for MUL and prod[63:32] for MULH/MULHU/MULHSU. This cuts DSP/LUT area\n     and the output mux fan-in on the longest remaining path.\n\n  3. rtl/ex_stage.sv: decode `div_op = in.valid && alu_op in {DIV,DIVU,REM,REMU}`;\n     instantiate `divider`; export `div_busy = div_op && !div_done` to the hazard unit\n     (via core.sv). Result mux: `ex_result = div_op ? div_result : alu_result`\n     (jump PC+4 override stays as is). EX/MEM register update: if (stall) hold; else if\n     (div_busy) load a bubble (`reg_q <= '0`: valid=0, ctrl=0 so reg_write/mem ops are\n     off and no forwarding from the bubble); else normal capture. For the DIV\n     instruction's own capture in the DONE cycle, take `rs1_val`/`rs2_val`\n     (RVFI-only fields) from the divider's latched a_q/b_q rather than the\n     current forwarding muxes, so rvfi_rs1_rdata/rs2_rdata match the Python ISS in\n     cosim; write_data/alu_result use the divider result. Under ALTOPS none of this\n     is active (done/busy tied to 0, div_op path inert).\n\n  4. rtl/hazard_unit.sv + rtl/core.sv: new input `div_busy`. `stall_if` and `stall_id`\n     gain `|| div_busy` (PC holds, ID/EX holds, IF/ID is a pure function of the held\n     PC so nothing is lost); `flush_id = (load_use || redirect) && !dmem_stall && !div_busy`.\n     `stall_ex_mem` and `hold_mem_wb` are unchanged: when the divider is busy the\n     EX/MEM register takes a bubble and the older instruction in EX/MEM flows to\n     MEM/WB normally. Once the bubble sits in EX/MEM, dmem_stall can no longer\n     assert (no mem op in EX/MEM), so the only dmem/div overlap is the start cycle,\n     which `start` explicitly guards with !stall_ex_mem (if the older mem op is still\n     waiting on the bus, the DIV simply waits in IDLE, busy=1, EX/MEM held).\n     redirect cannot fire while a DIV is in EX, so the IF redirect-priority logic is\n     untouched.\n\n  5. Tests: test/test_alu.py keeps its non-DIV vectors (the ALU no longer produces\n     DIV/REM in non-ALTOPS builds); move the existing DIV/REM vector set\n     (by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, signed/unsigned, back-to-back) into a new\n     test/test_divider.py that clocks the divider and waits for `done`, and add a\n     pipeline test (test_pipeline.py) with DIV followed by a dependent ADD and a\n     load-then-DIV so forwarding-before-latch and the dmem-stall start guard are\n     covered. Invariant 5b (M-ext correctness) is thereby still covered by cocotb +\n     cosim, and ALTOPS formal keeps proving bypass/stall behaviour unchanged.\n\n  Risks: (a) the first-cycle forwarding correctness (mitigated by latching at the\n  first non-dmem-stalled cycle, which is exactly the cycle baseline would have\n  committed that operand); (b) the new `busy` signal fans out to PC / ID/EX / EX/MEM\n  enables - it is derived from the ID/EX alu_op bits and a 2-bit FSM state, so it is\n  a 2-3 LUT path, far below the multiplier path; (c) if CoreMark's timed region\n  contains more divisions than expected, ~34 cycles each are charged to cycles, but\n  even 1000 divides would be <1% of the 4.49M-cycle run.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/divider.sv\n    description: \"New radix-2 restoring serial divider (IDLE/PREP/RUN/FIX/DONE FSM, latched raw operands, sign and by-zero/overflow fix-up); inert (busy=done=0) under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Remove combinational / and % from the non-ALTOPS branch; replace the three 64-bit multipliers with one shared signed 33x33 multiply with op-derived sign-extension bits. ALTOPS branch unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate divider, decode div_op, mux divider result into alu_result, drive div_busy, insert a bubble into EX/MEM while busy, feed RVFI rs1_val/rs2_val from the divider's latched operands for DIV-class ops.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add div_busy input: stall_if/stall_id |= div_busy and flush_id gated by !div_busy.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire div_busy from ex_stage to hazard_unit (new port on ex_stage, new port on hazard_unit).\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Drop DIV/REM vectors from the combinational ALU test (moved to the divider test); keep MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU vectors to guard the shared-multiplier rewrite.\"\n  - file: test/test_divider.py\n    description: \"New clocked cocotb test for divider.sv: signed/unsigned DIV/REM vectors, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, back-to-back ops, and random vectors vs a Python reference.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add DIV/REM pipeline tests: DIV then dependent consumer, producer-then-DIV forwarding, load-then-DIV, DIV with random dmem/imem stalls.\"\n", "structuredPatch": [{"oldStart": 31, "oldLines": 10, "newStart": 31, "newLines": 11, "lines": ["      -> RUN (32 cycles of restoring shift/subtract: rem = {rem[30:0], q[31]};", "      if rem >= div then rem -= div, q = {q[30:0],1} else q = {q[30:0],0}; one", "      33-bit subtract + mux per cycle) -> FIX (1 cycle: conditional negate of quotient", "-     or remainder, plus the b==0 and INT_MIN/-1 special cases: DIV by 0 -> all ones,", "-     DIVU by 0 -> all ones, REM/REMU by 0 -> dividend, DIV INT_MIN/-1 -> INT_MIN,", "-     REM INT_MIN/-1 -> 0; the plain restoring algorithm already yields q=all-ones and", "-     r=a for b==0 on unsigned magnitudes, but the sign fix-up must be bypassed) ->", "+     or remainder. RV32M corner cases: the plain restoring algorithm on magnitudes", "+     already yields q=all-ones and r=|a| for b==0, and q=0x80000000 / r=0 for", "+     INT_MIN/-1 after the normal sign fix-up, so the only explicit special case is", "+     DIV (signed) by 0, where the quotient sign fix-up must be bypassed so the result", "+     is all-ones rather than negated) ->", "      DONE (result valid, busy=0; return to IDLE when the instruction advances out of", "      EX, i.e. !stall_ex_mem). The latched operands make the result immune to the", "      forwarding selects changing while EX is parked (the producer retires while the"]}], "userModified": false, "replaceAll": false}}
