{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbEPKVctVQRCjmHjKGp3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the hypothesis to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-001-r1s0.yaml`. It passes `schemas/hypothesis.schema.json`. I did not run any simulation or timing, so the Fmax gain is an estimate.\n\n**Proposal:** replace the combinational DIV/REM logic in `alu.sv` with a serial divider that stalls EX for about 34 cycles, and share a single multiplier.\n\n**Why:** the baseline's worst path is 199.8 ns through about 150 logic levels, from MEM/WB `rd` through the forwarding unit and the ALU. I think only the four combinational 32-bit `/` and `%` array dividers can be that deep, though I did not confirm it in the netlist. They would also account for most of the 12.3K LUT4s. As far as I know CoreMark's timed region barely executes any divides, so a slow serial divider should cost almost no cycles.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`rtl/divider.sv` (new):**\n  - It is a radix-2 restoring divider that latches the raw operands on its first cycle, so forwarding changes during the stall can't corrupt the result.\n  - The latched operands also feed `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rs2_rdata` for cosim. That logic only feeds RVFI, so synthesis should prune it from the timed netlist.\n  - It is inert (`busy=done=0`) under `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, so formal behaves as before.\n- **`rtl/alu.sv`:**\n  - It drops `/` and `%` from the non-ALTOPS branch and keeps the ALTOPS branch unchanged.\n  - It replaces the three 64-bit multipliers with one shared signed 33x33 multiplier.\n- **`rtl/ex_stage.sv`, `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`, `rtl/core.sv`:**\n  - `div_busy` stalls the PC and ID/EX and inserts a bubble into EX/MEM.\n  - The divider only starts when EX/MEM isn't waiting on dmem.\n- **Tests:**\n  - `test_alu.py` loses its DIV/REM vectors, which move to a new clocked `test_divider.py`.\n  - `test_pipeline.py` gets DIV forwarding and stall cases.\n