{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbERypmaumnvnqSojap6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6502,"cache_read_input_tokens":76170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6502},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b1e97de-d89b-4a8a-930a-7e570581fb2d","uuid":"a7655e60-adf3-4faa-994c-b0b03844f5fa","timestamp":"2026-10-01T08:51:50.476Z","request_id":"req_011CfbERyMVDb2HHoCkNwSMS"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbERypmaumnvnqSojap6", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01NtDivvG4Kd4hifvzMjfeM8", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-sonnet-5-5_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261001-003-r1s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261001-003-r1s2.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20261001-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-stage predecode predictor: JAL always-taken + 256-entry bimodal branches, EX-verified\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Every taken branch and every JAL/JALR currently resolves in EX and costs one\n  bubble (the wrong-path instruction sitting in IF/ID is killed and the PC is\n  redirected a cycle late). I traced the CoreMark binary on the reference ISS\n  (test/cosim/reference.py): 2,937,037 retired instructions, of which 532k are\n  conditional branches (296k taken), 49.6k JAL and 13.8k JALR, i.e. 0.122\n  taken redirects per instruction. Against the measured ~1.53 CPI\n  (4,491,485 cycles for 10 iterations) those bubbles are ~8% of all cycles.\n  The baseline critical path (199 ns, the combinational multiply/divide ALU\n  through fwd_unit/ex_stage, Fmax 5.44 MHz) leaves ~180 ns of slack on every\n  fetch/branch path, so a fetch-side predictor is free in Fmax terms: the only\n  thing it can change is cycles per iteration. A misprediction costs exactly\n  what a taken branch costs today (1 bubble), so the scheme only removes\n  cycles; it cannot regress IPC except for predicted-taken/actually-not-taken\n  branches, which I included in the estimate below.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a predictor in if_stage that exploits the fact that IF and ID are\n  merged (imem_data arrives in the same cycle as imem_addr = pc), so the\n  fetched word can be predecoded and the next PC chosen in that cycle, with\n  no BTB, no tags and no aliasing of targets:\n    * JAL (opcode 1101111): always predicted taken, target = pc + J-imm\n      (one 32-bit adder in IF, fed from imem_data).\n    * Conditional branch (opcode 1100011): direction = MSB of a 2-bit\n      saturating counter from a 256-entry bimodal table indexed by pc[9:2];\n      target = pc + B-imm. Only predict taken if target[1:0] == 0 (keeps\n      imem_addr aligned for symbolic misaligned-branch streams). The table is\n      a plain array (no reset needed for correctness; any initial contents\n      are verified away by EX, and an un-reset array maps to LUT-RAM), written\n      only from EX when a valid conditional branch leaves EX\n      (in.valid && is_branch && !stall_ex_mem, so a dmem-stalled branch\n      trains once) with the actual taken bit.\n    * JALR and everything else: predicted not-taken (pc+4), unchanged.\n  next_pc in if_stage becomes pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. `redirect`\n  still has priority over everything and `stall` still holds the PC, so the\n  existing hazard_unit wiring is unchanged; pred_taken is only consumed when\n  the IF slot holds a real instruction (not stalled/flushed) and is zeroed in\n  the bundle for flushed/NOP slots. if_id_t and id_ex_t (core_pkg.sv) carry\n  pred_taken and pred_target; id_stage latches them (zero on reset/flush).\n  In ex_stage the resolved next PC (the expression already used for\n  reg_q.pc_next: misalign ? pc+4 : jump ? jump_target : taken ?\n  branch_target : pc+4) is compared with the predicted next PC\n  (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4); redirect = in.valid && mismatch and\n  redirect_target = the resolved next PC. This verification makes aliasing,\n  JALR, misaligned-target and any non-branch cases safe, and RVFI\n  pc_wdata/pc_rdata continuity plus the misalign-trap behaviour stay\n  identical to the baseline because pc_next is still computed from the\n  resolved outcome, never from the prediction. A mispredict squashes the one\n  wrong-path instruction in IF/ID exactly as today. Offline replay of the\n  2.94M-instruction CoreMark control-flow trace gives: JAL 100% correct,\n  conditional-branch bimodal-256 ~87% accurate, net 0.102 redirect bubbles\n  removed per instruction (account for predicted-taken/actually-not-taken\n  costs) = ~84% of all baseline redirects, i.e. ~6.7% fewer cycles\n  (~7% more iterations/cycle). Discounting ~7% of that for redirects that\n  overlap a dmem stall today, I expect ~+6% fitness with Fmax unchanged,\n  because the new IF adder/mux and EX 32-bit compare are tens of ns against\n  the ~199 ns ALU path that sets Fmax. (Replay also shows a 4-entry RAS adds\n  only ~0.3% and gshare adds <0.3%, so both are left out to keep the change\n  small and low-risk.)\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add pred_taken (1b) and pred_target (32b) to if_id_t and id_ex_t (zero in bubbles/flushed slots).\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Predecode imem_data (JAL / BRANCH); add 256x2b bimodal counter array indexed by pc[9:2] with a write port driven from EX, the pc+J/B-imm adder, and next_pc = pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4; drive pred_taken/pred_target on the IF/ID bundle (zero when flush/redirect/imem stall).\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch pred_taken/pred_target into the ID/EX register (zero on reset/flush).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute the resolved next PC once; redirect = in.valid && (resolved_npc != predicted_npc), redirect_target = resolved_npc; emit a train strobe (pc[9:2], taken) gated by in.valid && is_branch && !stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the EX train outputs back to if_stage; no top-level IO change.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6502, "cache_read_input_tokens": 76170, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6502}, "