al path (199 ns,\n  the combinational multiply/divide ALU through fwd_unit/ex_stage, Fmax 5.44\n  MHz) leaves ~180 ns of slack on every fetch/branch path, so a deeper\n  fetch-side predictor is completely free in Fmax terms: the only thing it can\n  change is cycles per iteration. A misprediction costs exactly what a taken\n  branch costs today (1 bubble), so the scheme can only gain cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a predictor in if_stage that exploits the fact that IF and ID are\n  merged (imem_data is available in the same cycle as imem_addr), so the\n  fetched word can be predecoded and the next PC chosen in the same cycle,\n  with no BTB tags and no aliasing:\n    * JAL: always predicted taken, target = pc + J-imm (adder in IF).\n    * Conditional branch: direction from a small bimodal table of 2-bit\n      saturating counters (64-128 entries, indexed by pc[k:2], updated only\n      from EX when a valid branch leaves EX, i.e. gated by in.valid and\n      !stall_ex_mem so a dmem-stalled branch trains once); target =\n      pc + B-imm. Only predict taken if target[1:0]==0.\n    * Return (JALR rd=x0, rs1 in {x1,x5}): target = top of a 4-entry return\n      address stack. The RAS is updated non-speculatively in EX (push pc+4\n      on JAL/JALR with rd in {x1,x5}; pop on return), so no wrong-path\n      repair logic is needed.\n  next_pc in if_stage becomes pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4 (redirect still\n  has priority over everything, and stall still holds the PC). if_id_t and\n  id_ex_t (core_pkg.sv) carry pred_taken and pred_target (the predicted\n  next PC); the bubble/flush paths zero them. In ex_stage the existing\n  resolved next-PC (the same expression already used for reg_q.pc_next:\n  misalign ? pc+4 : jump ? jump_target : taken ? branch_target : pc+4) is\n  compared against (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4); redirect =\n  in.valid && mismatch and redirect_target = the resolved next PC. This\n  verification makes every aliasing / stale-RAS / misaligned-target /\n  non-branch-hit case safe, and keeps RVFI pc_wdata/pc_rdata continuity and\n  the misalign-trap behaviour identical to the baseline because pc_next is\n  still computed from the resolved outcome, not the prediction. Mispredicts\n  squash the one wrong-path instruction in IF/ID exactly as today, so\n  hazard_unit's flush/stall wiring is unchanged. Expected: ~85-88%\n  direction accuracy on CoreMark's loop-dominated branches, ~all JALs and\n  most returns correct, which removes roughly 65-75% of the redirect\n  bubbles (~0.08-0.10 CPI, 5-7% fewer cycles per iteration) at no Fmax cost\n  because the new logic is far shorter than the ALU path that sets Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add pred_taken (1b) and pred_target (32b) to if_id_t and id_ex_t (zeroed in bubbles).\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Predecode imem_data (JAL / BRANCH / return JALR); add bimodal 2-bit counter table, 4-entry RAS and the pc+imm adder; next_pc = pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4; accept EX-side train/push/pop inputs; drive pred_* on the IF/ID bundle.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch pred_taken/pred_target into the ID/EX register (zero on reset/flush).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute resolved next PC once; redirect = valid && (resolved_npc != predicted_npc), redirect_target = resolved_npc; emit train (pc, is_branch, taken), call-push and return-pop strobes gated by in.valid && !stall && !misalign_fault.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the EX train/push/pop outputs back to if_stage; no top-level IO change.\"\n", "userModified": false}}
